WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:05.970
Most people have a sibling - in fact, children
in the U.S. today are more likely to grow

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:09.039
up with a brother or sister than with a father.

00:00:09.039 --> 00:00:15.199
Now, while a lot of social scientists research
the impact of schools and parenting, little

00:00:15.199 --> 00:00:20.330
attention is given to a relationship that
might be even more consequential.

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:26.710
So what do we know about siblings, birth order
and why children from one family grow up to

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:29.210
have very different personalities?

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:35.530
Let’s start with our analysis of the tragic
story of the two most famous brothers in Western

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:41.590
history.

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:47.070
Once upon a time there was a boy named Cain
who received all the love and attention from

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:48.920
both of his parents.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:54.260
Growing up, the boy knew only one world and
he himself was at its center.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:58.720
Everyone around him cared only for him and
nothing else.

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:04.480
All that changed when his mother gave birth
to Abel.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:09.480
Cain should have understood that his parents’
attention now had to be divided between him

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:10.500
and his brother.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:16.910
This was a revelation he wasn't able to accept
and he became increasingly jealous and sometimes

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:20.380
even violent against his little brother.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:26.810
Cain, as the first born, naturally looked
up to his father as his role model.

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:32.200
Following convention, the father himself raised
the boy with certain expectations.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:37.740
It was therefore especially painful for Cain
to see that his father now cared so much for

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:39.230
his younger brother.

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:43.490
Abel, naturally, looked up to his brother
for guidance.

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:51.280
Things were not easy for him either, but unlike
Cain, Abel knew no different.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:57.450
As the boys grew up, Cain gained a strong
sense of responsibility and, following tradition,

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.410
took over the family farm.

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:04.580
Abel, who was free from any obligation, chose
to become a shepherd.

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:10.049
Cain envied his younger brother’s autonomy,
because life as a farmer became increasingly

00:02:10.049 --> 00:02:12.950
difficult.

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:17.670
And on the day the brothers were to make a
gift to their father, Cain only brought a

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:20.120
few bundles of dry grass.

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:25.040
Abel, who did well for himself, brought an
entire lamb.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:31.700
When the father thanked Abel for his generosity,
Cain felt a deep shame.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:37.550
Overnight his shame turned to hatred of monstrous
proportions and the next day he killed his

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:41.709
own brother.

00:02:41.709 --> 00:02:47.689
The first big contribution to the field of
sibling research came from the Austrian psychiatrist

00:02:47.689 --> 00:02:52.680
Alfred Adler, and his theory of individual
psychology.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:58.290
Adler believed that the main motive of human
behavior is our desire for power, partly in

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:01.749
compensation for our feeling of inferiority.

00:03:01.749 --> 00:03:07.560
Hence, sibling dynamics play a central role
in family life and personality development

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:10.840
as we grow up.

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:16.620
Adler argued that children who grow up in
the same family experience the family differently.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:24.069
In particular, sibling rivalry over family
resources influences the development of personality.

00:03:24.069 --> 00:03:29.959
As a means of reducing competition, siblings
differentiate, develop different qualities

00:03:29.959 --> 00:03:32.500
and choose different interests.

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:38.650
When siblings don’t find their niches, their
relationship can suffer.

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:44.349
The Kellogg brothers hated each other, and
fought in court over who invented Corn Flakes.

00:03:44.349 --> 00:03:50.209
The Dasslers competed in business; after one
started Adidas, the other founded Puma.

00:03:50.209 --> 00:03:54.400
But many siblings love each other more than
anything.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.530
So what do we know about siblings in general?

00:03:57.530 --> 00:04:03.219
Here 6 things science suggests to be true:

00:04:03.219 --> 00:04:08.110
First born and single children look up to
their father or mother as role models.

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:13.340
The gap in mental and physical development
between them and their parents may be the

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:17.790
reason that they have, on average, a higher
IQ.

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:23.290
Parents tend to give their first born more
attention and often set higher expectations.

00:04:23.290 --> 00:04:28.590
If they can’t live up to those expectations,
they may feel inferior, but on average, they

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:33.020
seem to benefit from their position.

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:37.410
Younger siblings model their development after
their big brothers or sisters.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:40.800
They have a smaller developmental gap to overcome.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:45.630
They may also feel that they have less to
prove, but as a result of a lack of parental

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:51.570
attention they develop social anxiety more
often.

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:56.140
On average, Relationships between brothers
are more conflicted.

00:04:56.140 --> 00:05:01.110
Relationships between sisters are more intimate.

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:05.780
Siblings spend more time together than they
spend with their parents — younger siblings

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:10.030
even twice as much.

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:13.760
Children with siblings are physically more
active and less often obese.

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:16.780
They are often happier too.

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:21.720
Some studies suggest that they develop social
skills faster than children from only-child

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:27.320
families, but the research isn’t conclusive.

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:32.490
Sibling attachment seems to be a strong predictor
of well-being — sometimes more important

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:34.550
than paternal attachment.

00:05:34.550 --> 00:05:39.740
This also means that if one is bullying the
other, it can affect them for life.

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:44.960
One Swedish study of people aged 80 years
found that while closeness to friends did

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:52.320
little for total life satisfaction, feeling
close with a sibling did a lot.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:57.880
For those of you struggling with a difficult
sibling, Adler had the following advice: “A

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:02.260
simple rule in dealing with those who are
hard to get along with is to remember that

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:08.120
this person is striving to assert his superiority;
and you must deal with him from that point

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:33.330
of view.”

